/// \defgroup grp_w2_gui_window_text Window text: display strings, instance data and font release
/// \ingroup grp_gui_apt
/// \brief How a GUI window holds its text and tooltip, how those strings
///        are tracked and measured, and how font glyph data is released.
///
/// A window's text is not a plain string. Every GameWindow embeds a
/// WinInstanceData, the block of per-window settings that a .wnd layout or
/// a gadget factory fills in: id, style, status, owner, the colours and
/// images for each draw state, the font, the text and tooltip labels and the
/// tooltip delay. The text and the tooltip themselves are DisplayString
/// objects that WinInstanceData creates only when a window actually gets
/// text, through the display string manager (TheDisplayStringManager).
///
/// The pieces, and where each one is implemented:
/// - DisplayString is the device-independent half of a drawable string: a
///   UnicodeString, a GameFont and the links of the manager's list. Every
///   text edit (set, append, remove) ends in a notifyTextChanged call,
///   which the W3D subclass, W3DDisplayString, uses to recompute the
///   string's extents and to mark its render data for rebuilding.
///   Measuring and drawing are pure virtual here and live in
///   W3DDisplayString.
/// - The display string manager creates, tracks and frees display strings.
///   W3DDisplayStringManager::newDisplayString allocates a W3DDisplayString,
///   gives it the default font and adds it to the manager's list with
///   DisplayStringManager::link. Each W3DDisplayStringManager::update call
///   walks part of that list and frees the render data of strings that have
///   not been drawn for a while.
/// - WinInstanceData owns its window's two display strings and returns them
///   to the manager in WinInstanceData::init and in its destructor.
/// - Fonts are GameFont records kept by the font library (TheFontLibrary).
///   W3DFontLibrary attaches W3D glyph data to each one and releases it in
///   W3DFontLibrary::releaseFontData when the library deletes its fonts.
/// - GameWindowManager::winMakeColor packs a colour, and
///   GameWindowManager::winGetTextSize is the window system's (empty) text
///   measurement hook.
///
/// All five source files carry Zero Hour code. DisplayString.cpp and
/// DisplayStringManager.cpp were ported directly from the Zero Hour source;
/// WinInstanceData.cpp, W3DGameFont.cpp and GameWindowGlobal.cpp came
/// through BFME 1 donors. The BFME 2 differences are noted on each entity:
/// the changed DisplayString interface, the null guard in
/// DisplayStringManager::link and the longer display string manager
/// interface that WinInstanceData calls into.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Everything here is client-side GUI state; none of these functions
///   reads or writes game-logic state, so they play no part in lockstep
///   simulation. Whether BFME 2's multiplayer file checks cover .wnd
///   layouts or Language.ini has not been verified.
/// - What data controls: a window's text label, tooltip label, name, font,
///   text colours, image offset, draw-state images and colours, and tooltip
///   delay come from its .wnd layout. BFME 2's parsers for those fields
///   (TEXT, TOOLTIPTEXT, NAME, FONT, TEXTCOLOR, IMAGEOFFSET, the draw data
///   and TOOLTIPDELAY) are matched and store into WinInstanceData. Zero
///   Hour: when the layout is loaded, the text label is looked up in the
///   game's string table and the result becomes the window's text, except
///   for entry fields, which show the label itself. So wording and
///   translations are string-table edits, not layout edits.
/// - The default font of a new display string, and the font used for
///   characters the chosen font lacks, are language settings: BFME 2 reads
///   both from the global language data, which in Zero Hour come from the
///   DefaultDisplayStringFont and UnicodeFontName keys of Language.ini. With
///   no default font set, new strings use Times New Roman at 12 points;
///   without language data, the fallback glyph font is Arial Unicode MS.
///   Both names are hard-coded.
/// - Hard-coded: each draw state has nine image and colour slots (Zero
///   Hour's MAX_DRAW_DATA); a text label in a layout must be shorter than
///   128 characters or the parser rejects it; W3DFontLibrary::loadFontData
///   refuses fonts larger than 100 points; and WinInstanceData::init sets
///   the tooltip delay to -1, which Zero Hour's comment calls the default.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - The text renderer is a seam. DisplayString leaves measuring and drawing
///   to a subclass, and the manager decides which subclass to create, so a
///   new renderer supplies its own display string class and manager and
///   leaves windows and gadgets alone.
/// - Text is UTF-16: a WideChar is a 16-bit unsigned value, and editing
///   works one 16-bit unit at a time (DisplayString::appendChar,
///   DisplayString::removeLastChar). Characters outside the Basic
///   Multilingual Plane would be split by a backspace.
/// - Font glyph data is shared, reference-counted W3D data (Zero Hour: held
///   by the W3D asset manager). Zero Hour's FontCharsClass builds glyphs
///   from Windows GDI fonts at the requested point size, using a fixed 96
///   DPI, so the glyph cache itself ignores resolution. Resolution scaling
///   happens earlier: in BFME 2, callers such as the header template fonts,
///   the superweapon fonts, in-game UI messages, subtitles and named
///   timers, and the credits pass their size through
///   GlobalLanguage::adjustFontSize, which scales it by the horizontal
///   resolution relative to 1024 (Zero Hour used 800, with a Language.ini
///   adjustment factor and a clamp). Fonts requested without it, such as
///   the display string default, do not scale.
/// - Colours are packed 32-bit values with alpha in the top byte, then red,
///   green and blue (GameWindowManager::winMakeColor).
/// - In BFME 2 display strings are allocated with plain operator new rather
///   than a memory pool, and DisplayString has an ordinary virtual
///   destructor.
///
/// \see \ref sub_gui_apt


/// \class DisplayString
/// \ingroup grp_w2_gui_window_text
/// \brief A drawable piece of Unicode text: the string, its font, and the
///        links that put it on the display string manager's list.
///
/// DisplayString is the abstract base of the GUI's drawable text. Windows
/// hold their text and tooltip in one each (see WinInstanceData), and other
/// client code that draws text keeps its own. This base class stores the
/// text, the font and two list links; everything that needs a renderer
/// (word wrap, colours, measuring, drawing, clipping, hot-key highlighting) is
/// virtual and implemented by the W3D device layer's W3DDisplayString.
///
/// \par Ownership and lifetime
/// Display strings are created and freed by the display string manager,
/// never directly: W3DDisplayStringManager::newDisplayString allocates one,
/// sets its default font and links it into the manager's list with
/// DisplayStringManager::link. Zero Hour's free method unlinks the string,
/// clears the manager's update checkpoint if it pointed there, and deletes
/// it; BFME 2's has no matched row yet. The list links belong to the
/// manager; the string never touches them itself. The font is borrowed
/// from the font library and is not freed with the string.
///
/// \par The change notification
/// Every member that changes the text (DisplayString::setText,
/// DisplayString::appendChar, DisplayString::appendString,
/// DisplayString::removeLastChar) ends by calling notifyTextChanged. The
/// base version does nothing. W3DDisplayString overrides it to apply the
/// language's hard word-wrap setting, recompute the text extents, mark the
/// render data as changed and reset its two sentence renderers (the text
/// and the hot-key text), so the next draw rebuilds them.
/// DisplayString::reset clears the text without that notification.
///
/// \par Small members
/// getText returns a copy of the text, getTextLength its length in
/// characters, setFont and getFont set and return the font. setClipRegion
/// does nothing in the base class.
///
/// \note BFME 2 changes the interface, as the reconstruction reads it from
/// the retail virtual table. Zero Hour's DisplayString derives from
/// MemoryPoolObject and is freed through the memory pool; BFME 2's drops
/// that base, and its first virtual is an ordinary destructor. Zero Hour has
/// two draws, one taking colours and one taking colours plus drop-shadow
/// offsets. BFME 2 keeps one four-argument draw that the matched
/// W3DDisplayString body treats as a position plus drop-shadow offsets,
/// with the text and shadow colours supplied by three new colour setters
/// (setColors, setTextColor, setDropColor). It adds a draw that takes only
/// a position and forwards with zero offsets, which skips the shadow pass.
/// The parameter names in the reconstruction's DisplayString declaration
/// still follow Zero Hour's colour form. BFME 2 also adds two setters
/// taking a pair of floats whose purpose is not yet known, and
/// DisplayString::appendString.


/// \fn DisplayString::DisplayString(void)
/// \ingroup grp_w2_gui_window_text
/// \brief Creates an empty display string with no font and no list links.
///
/// The text starts empty; the font and both list links are cleared. In the
/// retail call graph the only caller is the W3DDisplayString constructor,
/// since the base class is abstract. The manager then sets the font and
/// links the string.
///
/// \see W3DDisplayStringManager::newDisplayString, DisplayStringManager::link


/// \fn DisplayString::~DisplayString(void)
/// \ingroup grp_w2_gui_window_text
/// \brief Destroys a display string after clearing its text and font.
///
/// Calls DisplayString::reset before the text member is destroyed. Because
/// this runs during destruction, it is the base class reset that runs, not
/// the W3D override. Retail callers are the W3DDisplayString destructor and
/// this class's deleting destructor.
///
/// The destructor does not unlink the string from the manager's list.
/// Strings must be freed through the display string manager; in Zero Hour
/// its free unlinks the string first (BFME 2's free has no matched row
/// yet). Deleting one directly would leave a dangling pointer in the list
/// that W3DDisplayStringManager::update walks.
///
/// \note Zero Hour declares its destructor through the memory pool glue;
///       BFME 2 has an ordinary virtual destructor in the first virtual
///       table slot.
/// \see DisplayString::reset


/// \fn void DisplayString::setText(UnicodeString text)
/// \ingroup grp_w2_gui_window_text
/// \brief Replaces the text, notifying the renderer only when it actually
///        changes.
///
/// This is how window text and tooltips are set:
/// WinInstanceData::setText and WinInstanceData::setTooltipText create the
/// display string on first use and then call this.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The new text is compared with the current one first. Only when they
/// differ is it copied and notifyTextChanged called. Setting the same text
/// every frame is therefore cheap: the W3D string keeps its extents and its
/// built sentence instead of recomputing them.
///
/// \param text The new text, passed by value. An empty string is allowed.
///
/// \see DisplayString::appendString, DisplayString::reset


/// \fn void DisplayString::reset(void)
/// \ingroup grp_w2_gui_window_text
/// \brief Clears the text and forgets the font, returning the string to
///        its freshly constructed state.
///
/// The text is emptied and the font pointer is set to null. The list links
/// are left alone, since they belong to the manager. Unlike the text
/// setters this does not call notifyTextChanged. Retail callers are
/// W3DDisplayString::reset, which calls it first and then clears its own
/// render state, and the destructor.
///
/// \note A string that is reset has no font. Code that reuses one must set
///       a font again before measuring or drawing it.
/// \see DisplayString::~DisplayString


/// \fn void DisplayString::appendChar(WideChar c)
/// \ingroup grp_w2_gui_window_text
/// \brief Appends one character to the end of the text.
///
/// This is the typing path: Zero Hour's text entry gadget appends each
/// character the player types this way. The text changes unconditionally
/// and notifyTextChanged is always called.
///
/// \param c The UTF-16 code unit to append. No filtering is done here;
///          character limits and allowed characters are the caller's job.
///
/// \note Retail inlines the one-character append of UnicodeString, and the
///       reconstruction writes it as a one-unit concatenation to match.
/// \see DisplayString::removeLastChar, DisplayString::appendString


/// \fn void DisplayString::appendString(const UnicodeString &text)
/// \ingroup grp_w2_gui_window_text
/// \brief Appends a whole string to the end of the text (BFME 2 only).
///
/// The string is concatenated onto the current text and notifyTextChanged
/// is called, exactly as DisplayString::appendChar does for one character.
/// No retail code calls it directly; it is reached through the virtual
/// table, and which callers use it is not yet established.
///
/// \param text The string to append. It is not modified.
///
/// \warning The name is an inference. The function is a BFME 2 addition
///          with no Zero Hour or BFME 1 counterpart, placed in the virtual
///          table right after appendChar, and was named from its shape. The
///          name may change.
/// \see DisplayString::appendChar, DisplayString::setText


/// \fn void DisplayString::removeLastChar(void)
/// \ingroup grp_w2_gui_window_text
/// \brief Removes the last character of the text, as a backspace does.
///
/// Removes the final UTF-16 code unit and calls notifyTextChanged. Zero
/// Hour's text entry gadget uses it for backspace.
///
/// \note It always notifies, even when the text was already empty, so the
///       W3D string recomputes its extents either way.
/// \see DisplayString::appendChar


/// \fn void DisplayStringManager::link(DisplayString *string)
/// \ingroup grp_w2_gui_window_text
/// \brief Puts a newly created display string at the head of the manager's
///        list of live strings.
///
/// The manager keeps every display string it has handed out on a doubly
/// linked list. W3DDisplayStringManager::newDisplayString calls this for
/// each string it creates. The list is how the manager finds strings later:
/// W3DDisplayStringManager::update walks up to ten strings per call,
/// starting where the previous call stopped, and frees the render data of
/// any string not drawn for more than 60 client frames, marking it to be
/// rebuilt if it is drawn again.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The string becomes the new head: its next link points at the old head,
/// and the old head's previous link points back at it. The new string's
/// previous link is not written; it is expected to be null already, as
/// DisplayString::DisplayString leaves it.
///
/// \param string The string to add. A null pointer is ignored.
///
/// \note Zero Hour asserts that the string is non-null and not already
///       linked. BFME 2 replaces the null assertion with an early return and
///       does not check the links.
/// \see W3DDisplayStringManager::newDisplayString


/// \fn void W3DFontLibrary::releaseFontData(GameFont *font)
/// \ingroup grp_w2_gui_window_text
/// \brief Drops the W3D glyph data attached to a font.
///
/// The font library keeps a list of GameFont records (in Zero Hour, one per
/// name, point size and boldness). When it deletes them all, which its
/// destructor does in BFME 2, it unlinks each font, calls this virtual to
/// release the device data, and then frees the record.
/// W3DFontLibrary::loadFontData is the other half: it gets the font's glyph
/// data through the glyph-cache lookup (Zero Hour: the W3D asset
/// manager's Get_FontChars; BFME 2 passes an extra font field to the
/// lookup whose meaning is not yet established) and also loads the
/// language's fallback Unicode font at the same size, attaching it to the
/// main glyph data for characters the main font lacks.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// When the font has glyph data, the fallback font's glyph data is released
/// first, if there is one, and then the font's own. Both are reference
/// counted, so this drops only the font's own references; data still held
/// elsewhere survives. The font's data pointer is then cleared.
///
/// \param font The font whose data to release. Must not be null: the null
///             check guards only the release, and the pointer is then
///             cleared unconditionally.
///
/// \note The original comment in this function says nothing needs to be
///       done because the asset manager handles fonts; the body does release
///       references. Both Zero Hour and BFME 2 behave as described here.
/// \see W3DFontLibrary::loadFontData


/// \class WinInstanceData
/// \ingroup grp_w2_gui_window_text
/// \brief The per-window settings block: identity, style, state, owner,
///        per-state colours and images, font, labels, tooltip delay, and the
///        window's text and tooltip display strings.
///
/// Each GameWindow embeds one of these as its instance data. The .wnd
/// layout parser and the gadget factories fill one in as a template, and
/// GameWindow::winSetInstanceData copies the template into the window.
/// Drawing code reads the colours and images for the window's current
/// state (enabled, disabled or highlighted) from it.
///
/// \par Contents
/// From the Zero Hour declaration, which BFME 2's matched bodies follow:
/// - window id, state bits, style bits, status bits and the owning window;
/// - three arrays of image, colour and border colour, one array per draw
///   state, with nine entries each;
/// - text and border colours for each state, plus a separate pair for
///   input-method composition text;
/// - an image offset and the font;
/// - four AsciiStrings: the text label and tooltip label from the layout,
///   the decorated window name from the GUI editor, and the name of a
///   header template for the font;
/// - the tooltip delay, the text and tooltip display strings, and a video
///   buffer pointer.
///
/// \par Ownership
/// The two display strings are owned: they are created on demand through
/// TheDisplayStringManager by setText and setTooltipText, and returned to
/// it by WinInstanceData::init and the destructor. The font, the images,
/// the owner window and the video buffer are borrowed and never freed here.
/// Zero Hour's comments say the video buffer belongs to the control that
/// plays the video and cannot be transferred to another window.
///
/// \par Copying
/// Because the display strings are owned, a plain copy would make two
/// windows share and later double-free them. The class has no hand-written
/// assignment: it relies on the one the compiler generates, which copies
/// every field in declaration order, assigns the four AsciiStrings through
/// their own assignment operator and leaves the object's virtual table
/// pointer alone. Its one retail user, GameWindow::winSetInstanceData,
/// repairs the result: it saves the window's own two display strings,
/// assigns the new data, puts the saved pointers back and clears the copied
/// video buffer. It then copies the source's text into the window's text
/// string when the source has non-empty text, and the source's tooltip into
/// the tooltip string when the source has a non-empty tooltip; otherwise
/// the window's existing display string keeps its previous text. Applying a
/// template with no text therefore does not clear a window's label.
///
/// \warning Assigning instance data anywhere other than through the
///          winSetInstanceData pattern copies the display string pointers
///          as they are, so both objects would free the same strings.
///
/// \par Small members
/// The inline accessors return the style, status, state, owner, font and
/// display strings, and getText, getTooltipText and their length variants
/// return empty values when the matching display string has not been
/// created. setText and setTooltipText create the display string if needed
/// and set its text.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's header warns that every new field must also be handled
///       in GameWindow::winSetInstanceData.


/// \fn WinInstanceData::WinInstanceData(void)
/// \ingroup grp_w2_gui_window_text
/// \brief Creates instance data with default values and no display
///        strings.
///
/// Clears the text, tooltip and video buffer pointers and then calls
/// WinInstanceData::init. The pointers must be cleared first because init
/// frees any display string it finds. Retail callers are the GameWindow
/// constructor, for the window's own copy, a constructor of one Apt screen
/// class whose name is not yet established, and code that builds a local
/// template and destroys it on return: the slider, text entry and combo box
/// factories, the list box scroll bar builder and the in-game palantir HUD
/// setup.
///
/// \see WinInstanceData::init


/// \fn WinInstanceData::~WinInstanceData(void)
/// \ingroup grp_w2_gui_window_text
/// \brief Returns the text and tooltip display strings to the display
///        string manager.
///
/// Each display string that exists is freed through
/// TheDisplayStringManager; the video buffer pointer is cleared but not
/// freed. Called when a GameWindow is destroyed, when the local templates
/// built by the gadget factories go out of scope, and through the class's
/// deleting destructor.
///
/// \note The free goes through BFME 2's longer display string manager
///       interface; the reconstruction calls it through a local view of
///       that class. The behaviour is Zero Hour's.
/// \see WinInstanceData::init


/// \fn void WinInstanceData::init(void)
/// \ingroup grp_w2_gui_window_text
/// \brief Resets every setting to its default and frees the display
///        strings.
///
/// The constructor calls it, and the gadget factories call it again to
/// reuse one stack template for several child windows: the combo box
/// factory, for example, re-initialises the same template before building
/// its drop-down button, its text entry and its list box.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Every image in the three draw-state arrays is cleared and every colour
///   and border colour set to the undefined colour; the same goes for the
///   enabled, disabled and highlighted text colours.
/// - Id, state, style and owner are cleared and the status set to none.
/// - The text label, tooltip label and decorated name are emptied and the
///   tooltip delay set to -1.
/// - The image offset and font are cleared, any existing text and tooltip
///   display strings are freed and their pointers cleared, and the video
///   buffer pointer is cleared.
///
/// Not touched: the composition text colours and the header template name,
/// in Zero Hour and in BFME 2 alike. A template reused after init keeps the
/// header template name of its previous use.
///
/// \see WinInstanceData::WinInstanceData


/// \fn Color GameWindowManager::winMakeColor(UnsignedByte red, UnsignedByte green, UnsignedByte blue, UnsignedByte alpha)
/// \ingroup grp_w2_gui_window_text
/// \brief Packs red, green, blue and alpha bytes into the window system's
///        Color value.
///
/// A virtual hook that lets a game choose its colour representation; this
/// engine uses GameMakeColor, so the result is alpha in the top byte, then
/// red, green and blue. It has no direct callers in retail, as expected for
/// a virtual.
///
/// \param red   Red component, 0 to 255.
/// \param green Green component, 0 to 255.
/// \param blue  Blue component, 0 to 255.
/// \param alpha Alpha component, 0 to 255; 255 is opaque.
/// \return The packed colour.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The body is Zero Hour's, ported through BFME 1. Its retail identity
/// rests on the compiled body placing at a single address in retail and on
/// its position in the window manager's virtual table, next to
/// winFindImage.
/// \see GameWindowManager::winGetTextSize


/// \fn void GameWindowManager::winGetTextSize(GameFont *font, UnicodeString text, Int *width, Int *height, Int maxWidth)
/// \ingroup grp_w2_gui_window_text
/// \brief Placeholder text-measurement hook: always reports a size of zero.
///
/// The window system declares this as the way to measure text in a font,
/// but the body only writes zero through whichever of the two output
/// pointers is non-null. Zero Hour's own comment marks it as unfinished:
/// real measurement goes through display strings (getSize and getWidth on
/// W3DDisplayString). Zero Hour's W3D window border drawing
/// (W3DGameWindow::winDrawBorder) still calls it to measure an entry
/// field's label, reserving space beside the field, and so always gets
/// zero; the text entry gadget's call sits in commented-out IME code. BFME
/// 2 has no direct named caller.
///
/// \param font     Ignored.
/// \param text     Ignored. Passed by value, so each call copies the string.
/// \param width    [out] Set to 0 when not null.
/// \param height   [out] Set to 0 when not null.
/// \param maxWidth Ignored.
///
/// \note Retail BFME 2 keeps this body in the window manager's virtual
///       table, after winMakeColor and winFindImage, as Zero Hour declares
///       it. The reconstruction declares it non-virtual, following the BFME 1
///       header. The declaration may change when the class is reconciled.
/// \par Remastering
/// A port that needs real text extents here should measure through a
/// display string instead of relying on this hook.
/// \see GameWindowManager::winMakeColor
